serene and duncan smith who's supporting  liz trust well firstly you must be  delighted with the result yeah i'm very  pleased uh our candidate liz trust put  on 27 extra which was the biggest growth  of all of the three candidates  and she's through now to the round with  the public to the members so that's very  good news she's bought a very good  campaign i'm  sorry the others have fallen out we're  all conservatives at the end of the day  i hope we'll all come back together  again and just get on the government as  and when whoever wins the final round  you just say you know there that we're  all conservatives we need to kind of  pull together and beth ripley was just  making the point that i guess liz trust  and rishi sunak have really been getting  stuck into each other a little bit in  those tv debates so whoever wins is  gonna have in her words a hell of a job  turning it around we always say that  i've done seven of these one two and one  one myself and everybody always says  will you come back together again and  almost always we come back together  again i mean these debates are big  debates and i think the public has a  right to hear them what are you going to  do about helping people who are  suffering from the cost of living crisis  should we get taxes down do we need to  have such high taxes i don't think so  these are big debates that the public  needs to see us and recognize we're  passionate about and we may disagree on  some of these things but at the end of  the day we have one person all  conservatives want to get the basic tax  down the question is when do you do it  but you also will acknowledge as you  know you are you know a veteran if you  like of some of these debates that there  have been a personal attacks i mean you  for example have been  you know told me about penny more than  what she's done for the last few years  we've seen lord frost for example  questioning her comments there have been  clearly  a tax page they're not personal attacks  if you ask i want to know i mean i uh  judged everybody on what their record  was in government over two and a half  years you had two and a half years to  deliver brexit get things done who did  the most who actually managed to get  those across the line and i went to lose  trust because she had the strongest  record on it  my question about penny morton was i  don't know what she's done she needs to  be clear about what her successes and  failures were how did she battle things  over that's all so just a reasonable  question that's not an attack on anybody  do you think the tone of the debates  will soften in the months ahead over  some of them well i think they'll be  they're going to be tight because  it's a very important job and the  direction of the party and the country  will depend on who wins it  so  my view is that they will be quite  fierce  but i don't think they're going to get  personal i don't think you know i would  advise everybody  policy yes  debates about how you do stuff but stay  off the personal stuff on the policy  though we now have two very very  different visions of how the uk should  be run uh on the ballot paper don't we  uh we have uh rishi sunak and liz trust  and if you look at everything from you  know taxation to spending  we can't be ending up with two very  different visions of britain well there  are differences but their differences  really of emphasis and of timing  even rishi soon act says he wants to  bring taxes down but he doesn't believe  you do it now i disagree with him i  think he's got the headroom to do it liz  believes that  there are economic commentators out  there that actually agree with us and  some that agree with him  this is a serious debate to be had  because people's lives depend on what we  now do so i think these are legitimate  debates  the whole conservative party wants to  bring the base of taxation down the  question is when and how you do it which  is what's going to be debated uh what i  do want to ask you now is that the next  prime minister is going to be decided by  conservative party members  somewhere between 160 000 or 200 000  people right  whoever wins should they not call a  general election so they then have a  mandate to implement their vision which  as we know is going to be quite  different from the mandate the manifesto  of the last days no because  we don't have a presidential system here  so we have a parliamentary system  the prime minister is only the leader of  the party while he holds their  confidence  we've had this going down hundreds of  years where they don't hold their  confidence they have to go and somebody  else who has the confidence of the  political party that was elected in this  case conservatives  has a right to see out that term so i  don't think a general election is either  wanted or warranted what we need to do  is go to the next period for two years  have the election then people can judge  us on what we've done i thought we lost  two and a half years because of the  covid crisis now we have to get those  things sorted out  be madness to go to an election i know  some people will say an election tall  people want elections all the time some  people but the answer is you need  stability right now and that's what this  is all about how concerned are you that  as the you know country faces the cost  of living crisis you know there's a war  in ukraine  and now we really have a bit of a vacuum  in august uh as british sooner and liz  trusts slug it out  well there's lots that can be done right  now by the government still run by boris  johnson there's huge things they've got  on their agenda they can do and there's  lots they can do to help people out in  the meantime  this debate will come to fruition at the  end of august and what will happen is a  new administration  but now with a different type of energy  and focused on the things that we want  the decisions about taxation are what  this is really all about  how 